
Diagnose why a second integration on the same SystemSolver fails. IDASolve
returns IDA_ERR_FAIL (-3) on the first step of the second run, whether via a
second runSolver() or a second initialize()/integrate() pair, and whether or not
the first run completed. Pre-existing -- b7d8031 fails identically -- and hidden
because PyRunner::configure builds a fresh SystemSolver every time. Candidates:
state a completed run leaves in RF_cellwise / updateBoundaryConditions, and the
nc_output time index. Tests/README.md has the detail.

Design a dG/dt early-exit gate for optimisation runs. origin/optimize-mode has
one (commit e024294) and the initialize/integrate/destroySundials split exists
partly to serve it, but it was not ported: it evaluates the objective functional
on the time derivative, which equals dG/dt only if g is linear in the state; it
scales the result by the netCDF output cadence, so an I/O setting decides whether
a step is rejected (and setOutputCadence(0.0) disables the gate silently); and
its thresholds are hardcoded. Wants an explicit dG/dt hook on AdjointProblem and
a configurable tolerance.

From the origin/optimize-mode review, still unmerged and worth taking:
  - shard_inputs as a per-hook decorator in python/Stellarator2.py (but keep
    MaNTA_Decorator sharding, or every hook needs the new decorator)
  - the Python drivers: StellaratorState.py, desc-optimize-bfgs.py, scan_eq.py,
    scan_eq_ambipolar.py, yancc_gpu_test.py
  - why the branch removed both `#pragma omp parallel for` from
    TransportSystem.hpp's batched defaults -- ask eatocco; may be a real finding
    about OpenMP against a Python trampoline
